<p>A theme decides what the public site looks like. The administration has its own, fixed theme - a broken site theme can never lock anyone out of it.</p>

<h2>Choosing</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Themes</strong>, the active theme is chosen; only one is active at a time. The default theme, <code>default</code>, is there from the start. More come from <strong>Packages</strong>, for example <a href="https://github.com/alex01at/modulento-theme-indigo">Indigo</a>, a calm, light marketplace theme, or a trade-specific one such as Audigo for auction houses. Another theme usually only contains what it changes compared to the default theme.</p>

<h2>Branding</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Themes → Branding</strong>: the logo (also a variant of its own for the dark colour scheme), the favicon, and whether the site follows the device or stays light or dark always.</p>

<h2>Theme and design are two different things</h2>
<p>The <strong>design</strong> settings (chapter "Pages and content") fine-tune a theme's colours, font and corner radius, without changing its layout. A different <strong>theme</strong>, on the other hand, chooses a fundamentally different layout for the site.</p>
